<article class="post-body">

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
  <div class="section-no">01 — The Encoder Tax</div>
  <h2>The Multimodal Architecture Mismatch</h2>
  <p>Traditional multimodal models like LLaVA or Qwen2-VL are built by gluing together pre-existing, frozen sensory encoders (like a Vision Transformer or a Conformer ASR model) to an LLM backbone. While convenient, this approach levies a heavy <strong>"encoder tax"</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dual-Stage Latency:</strong> The input image or audio sequence must complete its forward pass through a deep, multi-layer encoder <em>before</em> the LLM decoder can even begin its first projection.</li>
    <li><strong>Bloated Memory Footprint:</strong> Encoders add hundreds of millions of parameters (often 300M to 600M) that consume GPU memory, which is critical for local inference.</li>
    <li><strong>Representation Bottleneck:</strong> Freezing the encoder forces a mismatch. A separate projection adapter must learn to compress high-level semantic tokens into the LLM's residual stream, limiting the granularity of raw perception.</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p><strong>Gemma 4 12B solves this by going completely encoder-free.</strong> Raw pixel patches and acoustic frames bypass heavy stacks and are projected straight into the LLM's unified hidden dimension. Use the interactive pipeline below to contrast these two paradigms.</p>

