<!-- SECTION 2 (BARRIER) -->
<section id="s-barrier" data-code="barrier">
  <div class="section-no">02 — The Deletion Barrier</div>
  <h2>Why Deleting the Encoder is the Holy Grail (and Why it Kept Failing)</h2>
  <p>Every AI hardware and software engineer wants to delete the heavy vision encoder. Pretrained encoders like CLIP or SigLIP add hundreds of millions of parameters, consume valuable VRAM, restrict input resolutions, and add serial latency during inference. Yet, until recently, almost every attempt to build an encoder-free vision-language model resulted in severe degradation of visual reasoning quality. Three major barriers stood in the way:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Representation Gap:</strong> Pretrained vision encoders are deep neural networks trained on billions of images to extract abstract semantic features. When you remove the encoder and feed raw pixel patches straight into the LLM's early layers, the LLM has to learn low-level visual processing (detecting edges, shapes, textures, lighting) from scratch. Unrefined pixel data acts as continuous high-variance noise, disrupting the pretrained text layers.</li>
    <li><strong>The Context Window Blowup:</strong> Vision encoders compress large spatial grids into a compact set of tokens. Without this compression, feeding raw image patches directly into the transformer blows up the sequence length. For example, a high-resolution image projected at a $14 \times 14$ patch size produces thousands of tokens, saturating the LLM's $O(N^2)$ self-attention mechanism. Early models (like Fuyu) bypassed this by using larger patches ($30 \times 30$), but this destroyed visual details, making them fail at OCR and text-rich document retrieval.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradient Interference &amp; Modality Conflict:</strong> During training, backpropagating chaotic visual gradients through the shared weights of a text decoder degrades its established cognitive abilities. The model undergoes a form of "lobotomy"—gaining basic vision at the cost of losing its math, coding, and logical reasoning benchmarks.</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p><strong>Gemma 4 12B resolves these conflicts</strong> by utilizing a shallow 35M patch-projection adapter equipped with a gated activation mechanism (which starts at zero). This acts as a protective shield, buffering the core text-decoder parameters from visual noise during early training phases. Together with multi-token prediction (MTP) co-training, this allows Gemma 4 to achieve high-fidelity visual understanding without degrading text capabilities.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section id="s-vision" data-code="vision">
  <div class="section-no">03 — Vision Projection</div>
  <h2>The 35M Parameter Linear Vision Embedder</h2>
  <p>How do raw pixels enter the transformer without a Vision Transformer (ViT) encoding them? Gemma 4 12B maps them directly using a patch-slicing method:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Patching:</strong> The model divides the input image into <strong>48×48 pixel patches</strong> (in RGB, this flat vector is $48 \times 48 \times 3 = 6,912$ channels).</li>
    <li><strong>Matrix Multiplication:</strong> This 6,912-element vector undergoes a single linear projection matrix multiplication (W_proj ∈ ℝ^(6,912 × 4,096)) mapping it directly into the LLM's model dimension (d_model = 4,096).</li>
    <li><strong>Positional Embeddings:</strong> Since no self-attention happens inside the vision embedder, spatial orientation is encoded by adding factorized 2D position embeddings (x_pos + y_pos) to each patch token vector.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Click on different patches of the sample image below to see how they are flattened, projected, and labeled with their coordinates.</p>

