<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<section id="s-audio" data-code="audio">
  <div class="section-no">04 — Audio Frame-Slicing</div>
  <h2>ASR-Free Acoustic Processing</h2>
  <p>Historically, audio processing in LLMs required a deep acoustic model (like Whisper's transformer encoder) that downsampled and encoded raw waveforms. Gemma 4 12B processes audio natively, treating speech similar to text. The mathematical pipeline works as follows:</p>
  
  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Acoustic Slicing &amp; Projection</div>
    Waveform at 16 kHz ──> 40ms frame = 640 float samples<br/>
    Frame vector <span class="math-var">f</span> ∈ ℝ⁶⁴⁰<br/>
    Token vector <span class="math-var">t_audio</span> = LayerNorm(<span class="math-var">f</span> · <span class="math-var">W_audio</span>) ∈ ℝ⁴⁰⁹⁶
  </div>
  
  <p>By dividing the waveform into non-overlapping 40ms windows, a single linear matrix multiplication (W_audio ∈ ℝ^(640 × 4,096)) is sufficient to map audio signals directly into the model's vocabulary dimension. Slide the swept player window below to inspect how waveforms map directly to specific projected tokens.</p>

